\ ==== gc pressure ====

variable s
"" to s
: g ^s | 1500 begin s "x" + to s 1 - dup 0 = until drop ;
g
s size .

clear

\ ==== quoted string gc ====

\ Regression: a string literal inside an anonymous quotation must survive GC.
\ The quotation's CFA and body are emitted inline inside the enclosing word's
\ compiled body, between a (branch) that skips them at run time and a
\ (literal) that publishes the xt. mark_body must walk into the embedded body
\ cell by cell so the (dostr) op marks the template string.
\
\ A previous bug treated the embedded docol cell as a 2-cell call site and
\ skipped the first body cell of the quotation. When that first cell was a
\ (dostr) op, the template went unmarked, got swept, and the next invocation
\ of the quotation dereferenced a NULL Object and segfaulted.

: msg-xt  [: "msg" :] ;

msg-xt execute drop
gc
msg-xt execute drop
gc
.s

clear

\ ==== gc deep nesting ====

\ Marking a deeply nested value must not recurse on the native C stack:
\ mark_value recurses only to a fixed depth and then queues children on a
\ worklist, so an array nested 1M levels deep survives a collection instead
\ of crashing. Reverting to unbounded recursion turns this into a SIGSEGV.
[ ] [: 1 array :] 1000000 times gc drop 42 . cr

\ Non-tail nesting: each array holds the deeper one in element 0, not the
\ tail, so the tail loop cannot absorb it. Recursive marking on non-tail
\ children overflowed the C stack. mark descends a bounded number of C
\ frames, then defers deeper children to an explicit worklist, so arbitrary
\ nesting survives a collection instead of crashing.
[ 0 ] [: 0 2 array :] 200000 times gc 43 . cr

clear
